__registerContent("quickrecall/history/buddhism", {"notes":{"qr-buddhism":{"era":"Quick Recall — Ancient History","significance":["Buddhism is a very high-frequency Ancient History and Art & Culture topic — founder, teachings, councils and schools are repeatedly tested","The Four Noble Truths, the Eightfold Path, the Buddhist Councils and the Hinayana–Mahayana split are core exam facts","Links to Ashoka, Kanishka, the Tripitaka and Buddhist art/architecture make it cross-cutting"],"features":[{"key":"Origin & the Buddha","main":"Founder: Gautama Buddha.\nBorn: 563 BCE (Lumbini, Nepal); Died (Mahaparinirvana): 483 BCE (Kushinagar).\nChildhood name: Siddhartha; Father: Suddhodana; Mother: Mahamaya; foster-mother: Mahaprajapati Gautami; Wife: Yashodhara; Son: Rahula.\nClan: Shakya; born at Kapilavastu region.","note":"Buddhism is mentioned in the Tripitaka; spread widely under Emperor Ashoka."},{"key":"Key Life Events","main":"Four Sights (that deeply influenced him): an old man, a sick man, a dead man, and an ascetic (monk).\nMahabhinishkramana (Great Renunciation): left home at age 29 (his horse was Kanthaka, charioteer Channa).\nEnlightenment (Nirvana/Bodhi): attained at Bodh Gaya under a peepal tree.\nFirst sermon (Dharmachakra Pravartana): at Sarnath.","note":"Died at Kushinagar at the age of 80."},{"key":"Four Noble Truths (Arya Satya)","main":"1. There is suffering (Dukkha).\n2. There is a cause of suffering — desire/craving (Trishna).\n3. Suffering can be ended (cessation of craving is possible).\n4. The Eightfold Path (Ashtangika Marg) leads to the end of suffering.","note":"The Middle Path avoids extremes of indulgence and asceticism."},{"key":"Eightfold Path (Ashtangika Marg)","main":"Right View, Right Intention/Resolve (Sankalp), Right Speech (Vani), Right Action (Karm), Right Livelihood (Ajivika), Right Effort (Prayas), Right Mindfulness (Smriti), Right Concentration (Samadhi).","note":"Following this path leads to the cessation of suffering and Nirvana."},{"key":"Buddhist Councils","main":"1st Council — Rajgriha (under King Ajatashatru).\n2nd Council — Vaishali (under King Kalashoka).\n3rd Council — Pataliputra (under Emperor Ashoka).\n4th Council — Kashmir (under Kanishka); Buddhism split into Hinayana and Mahayana here.","note":"The 4th Council (Kanishka) formalised the Mahayana development."},{"key":"Tripitaka & Schools","main":"Tripitaka ('three baskets'), written in PALI:\n  Vinaya Pitaka — monastic rules & discipline.\n  Sutta (Sutra) Pitaka — teachings/sermons.\n  Abhidhamma (Abhidharma) Pitaka — philosophy.\nSchools: Hinayana (Theravada — follows old teachings; regards Buddha as a teacher) and Mahayana (regards Buddha as God; idol worship; Bodhisattva ideal).","note":"Buddha, Dhamma and Sangha are the 'Three Jewels' (Triratna)."},{"key":"Symbols of Buddhism","main":"Wheel (Dharmachakra) — the first sermon/teaching.\nLotus (Kamal) — birth.\nStupa — death/Mahaparinirvana.\nBodhi tree — enlightenment.\nHorse (Kanthaka) — renunciation.","note":"The Buddha was not depicted in human form in early (Hinayana) art — symbols were used."}],"traps":[{"label":"Dates and places","lines":["Born 563 BCE (Lumbini); died 483 BCE (Kushinagar) at age 80.","Enlightenment: Bodh Gaya; First sermon: Sarnath."]},{"label":"Councils and kings","lines":["1st Rajgriha (Ajatashatru); 2nd Vaishali (Kalashoka); 3rd Pataliputra (Ashoka); 4th Kashmir (Kanishka).","The Hinayana–Mahayana split occurred at the 4th Council under Kanishka."]},{"label":"Symbols mapping","lines":["Lotus = birth; Bodhi tree = enlightenment; Wheel = first sermon; Stupa = death; Horse = renunciation.","Tripitaka is written in PALI."]}],"mainsAngle":"Buddhism arose as a reformist response to the ritualism and rigidity of later Vedic society, offering an ethical, rational Middle Path accessible across castes. Under royal patronage (Ashoka, Kanishka) it became a pan-Asian religion, deeply shaping Indian art, architecture, education (Nalanda) and philosophy.","mainsTheme":"Buddhism — reform movement, royal patronage and cultural influence","recall":["Founder: Gautama Buddha; born 563 BCE (Lumbini), died 483 BCE (Kushinagar)","Childhood name Siddhartha; Shakya clan; son Rahula, wife Yashodhara","Four Noble Truths + Eightfold Path (Middle Path)","Councils: Rajgriha → Vaishali → Pataliputra → Kashmir","4th Council (Kanishka): Hinayana–Mahayana split","Tripitaka (Vinaya, Sutta, Abhidhamma) in Pali","Symbols: Lotus (birth), Bodhi tree (enlightenment), Wheel (first sermon), Stupa (death)"]}},"quizzes":{"qr-buddhism":{"title":"Buddhism (Bauddha Dharma)","questions":[{"q":"Gautama Buddha was born in 563 BCE at:","options":["Kushinagar","Lumbini","Bodh Gaya","Sarnath"],"correct":1,"explain":"Gautama Buddha was born in 563 BCE at Lumbini (in present-day Nepal)."},{"q":"The childhood name of Gautama Buddha was:","options":["Vardhamana","Siddhartha","Rahula","Gautami"],"correct":1,"explain":"His childhood name was Siddhartha; he belonged to the Shakya clan."},{"q":"Buddha attained enlightenment (Nirvana) at:","options":["Sarnath","Bodh Gaya","Kushinagar","Vaishali"],"correct":1,"explain":"Buddha attained enlightenment under a peepal (Bodhi) tree at Bodh Gaya."},{"q":"Buddha delivered his first sermon (Dharmachakra Pravartana) at:","options":["Lumbini","Sarnath","Rajgriha","Pataliputra"],"correct":1,"explain":"Buddha delivered his first sermon at Sarnath (near Varanasi)."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about Buddha's death:","I. He attained Mahaparinirvana at Kushinagar.","II. He died at the age of 80 in 483 BCE.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — Buddha died (Mahaparinirvana) at Kushinagar at the age of 80, in 483 BCE."},{"q":"How many Noble Truths (Arya Satya) form the core of Buddha's teaching?","options":["Three","Four","Five","Eight"],"correct":1,"explain":"There are Four Noble Truths, the last of which points to the Eightfold Path."},{"q":"According to the Four Noble Truths, the cause of suffering is:","options":["Ignorance","Desire/craving (Trishna)","Bad karma","Fate"],"correct":1,"explain":"The second Noble Truth identifies desire/craving (Trishna) as the cause of suffering."},{"q":"The Eightfold Path consists of how many components?","options":["Four","Six","Eight","Ten"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Ashtangika Marg (Eightfold Path) has eight components, e.g. Right View, Right Speech, Right Action, Right Concentration."},{"q":["Match the Buddhist Council with the king in whose reign it was held:","I. First Council (Rajgriha) — a) Ashoka","II. Third Council (Pataliputra) — b) Ajatashatru","III. Fourth Council (Kashmir) — c) Kanishka","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I-b, II-a, III-c","I-a, II-b, III-c","I-c, II-a, III-b","I-b, II-c, III-a"],"correct":0,"explain":"First Council: Ajatashatru (b); Third Council: Ashoka (a); Fourth Council: Kanishka (c)."},{"q":"At which Buddhist Council did Buddhism split into the Hinayana and Mahayana schools?","options":["First Council (Rajgriha)","Second Council (Vaishali)","Third Council (Pataliputra)","Fourth Council (Kashmir, under Kanishka)"],"correct":3,"explain":"The split into Hinayana and Mahayana is associated with the Fourth Council, held in Kashmir under Kanishka."},{"q":"The Tripitaka, the sacred texts of Buddhism, were composed in which language?","options":["Sanskrit","Prakrit","Pali","Magadhi"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Tripitaka (Vinaya, Sutta and Abhidhamma Pitakas) were composed in Pali."},{"q":["Match the Pitaka with its content:","I. Vinaya Pitaka — a) Philosophy","II. Sutta Pitaka — b) Monastic rules/discipline","III. Abhidhamma Pitaka — c) Teachings/sermons","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I-b, II-c, III-a","I-a, II-b, III-c","I-c, II-a, III-b","I-b, II-a, III-c"],"correct":0,"explain":"Vinaya = monastic rules (b); Sutta = teachings/sermons (c); Abhidhamma = philosophy (a)."},{"q":"The 'Great Renunciation' (Mahabhinishkramana) — Buddha leaving his home — occurred at what age?","options":["19","25","29","35"],"correct":2,"explain":"Siddhartha left his home at the age of 29 in search of truth (the Great Renunciation)."},{"q":["Consider the following 'Four Sights' that influenced Buddha:","I. An old man","II. A sick man","III. A dead man","IV. An ascetic","How many of the above are correct?"],"options":["Two","Three","Four","One"],"correct":2,"explain":"All four — an old man, a sick man, a dead man, and an ascetic — are the sights that deeply influenced Siddhartha."},{"q":["Match the Buddhist symbol with what it represents:","I. Lotus — a) Enlightenment","II. Bodhi tree — b) Birth","III. Stupa — c) Death (Mahaparinirvana)","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I-b, II-a, III-c","I-a, II-b, III-c","I-c, II-a, III-b","I-b, II-c, III-a"],"correct":0,"explain":"Lotus = birth (b); Bodhi tree = enlightenment (a); Stupa = death/Mahaparinirvana (c). The Wheel symbolises the first sermon and the horse renunciation."},{"q":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes Hinayana from Mahayana Buddhism?","options":["Hinayana worships the Buddha as God; Mahayana treats him as a teacher","Hinayana follows the older teachings and treats Buddha as a great teacher; Mahayana regards Buddha as God and permits idol worship","Both regard Buddha as God","Both reject the idea of Nirvana"],"correct":1,"explain":"Hinayana (Theravada) follows the older teachings and treats Buddha as a teacher; Mahayana regards Buddha as God and involves idol worship and the Bodhisattva ideal."},{"q":"The three 'Jewels' (Triratna) of Buddhism are:","options":["Buddha, Dhamma and Sangha","Ahimsa, Satya and Aparigraha","Vinaya, Sutta and Abhidhamma","Lumbini, Bodh Gaya and Sarnath"],"correct":0,"explain":"The Triratna (Three Jewels) are the Buddha, the Dhamma (teaching), and the Sangha (community of monks)."},{"q":"The foster-mother who raised Gautama Buddha was:","options":["Mahamaya","Yashodhara","Mahaprajapati Gautami","Trishala"],"correct":2,"explain":"After his mother Mahamaya died, Siddhartha was raised by his foster-mother Mahaprajapati Gautami."},{"q":"Which emperor is most associated with the spread of Buddhism across and beyond India?","options":["Chandragupta Maurya","Ashoka","Samudragupta","Harshavardhana"],"correct":1,"explain":"Emperor Ashoka played the greatest role in propagating Buddhism, sending missionaries across and beyond India."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about Gautama Buddha:","I. His wife was Yashodhara and son was Rahula.","II. He belonged to the Shakya clan.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — Buddha's wife was Yashodhara, his son Rahula, and he belonged to the Shakya clan."}]}}});
